WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:02.850
Film Courage: How do you know if an idea is
worth developing into a story?

00:00:02.850 --> 00:00:08.320
Patricia Vidal Delgado, Writer/Director: I
have a sounding board who is…I guess he's

00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:15.570
kind of my secret weapon…but he's someone
who has seen all of my short films and he's

00:00:15.570 --> 00:00:21.880
always the first person I go to with an idea
even at a treatment stage to be like Is this

00:00:21.880 --> 00:00:24.310
any good or is this really terrible?

00:00:24.310 --> 00:00:27.560
If it's really terrible can I salvage it?

00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:28.750
Is there a way to make it better?

00:00:28.750 --> 00:00:34.030
It's a very good friend called Faisal A. Qureshi.

00:00:34.030 --> 00:00:37.490
He's a professional screenwriter based in
the UK.

00:00:37.490 --> 00:00:39.730
He's also directed films.

00:00:39.730 --> 00:00:48.600
I think it's very important as a writer to
be able to show someone that you trust something

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:55.670
that you're not entirely sure if it's any
good or not because he knows me well enough

00:00:55.670 --> 00:00:59.210
now that he knows he can be really harsh and
it’s okay because at the end of the day

00:00:59.210 --> 00:01:02.620
I just want to know if I am wasting my time
or not.

00:01:02.620 --> 00:01:08.920
And he has been very harsh and I really appreciate
it because I have had instances where I have

00:01:08.920 --> 00:01:15.520
sent a treatment and he’ll tell me what
is wrong with it like in a very kind of brutal

00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:16.520
way.

00:01:16.520 --> 00:01:20.729
But then that’s good because it helps me
figure out how I can make it better and I

00:01:20.729 --> 00:01:24.950
have actually been able to make it better
and get onto fellowships with treatments that

00:01:24.950 --> 00:01:27.960
before he was like [makes face and shakes
head no] “Hmmm?

00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:29.220
I don’t think so?”

00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:34.149
Film Courage: Does he reciprocate where he
sends you his scripts?

00:01:34.149 --> 00:01:35.950
Patricia: He does, yes.

00:01:35.950 --> 00:01:39.530
He has sent me his materials before.

00:01:39.530 --> 00:01:40.530
So definitely.

00:01:40.530 --> 00:01:44.799
I think that it’s so important is to have
someone because I think I always feel like

00:01:44.799 --> 00:01:51.010
in this industry you only get one chance to
impress people so you want to put your best

00:01:51.010 --> 00:01:52.010
foot forward.

00:01:52.010 --> 00:01:58.230
That’s why it’s important to have someone
whose opinion you trust and whose craft you

00:01:58.230 --> 00:02:02.750
really admire and you recognize that they
are really talented and intelligent and you

00:02:02.750 --> 00:02:10.250
can show them your worst stuff and they will
give you suggestions and will tell you what

00:02:10.250 --> 00:02:12.240
you could do to make it a little bit better.

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:15.590
Film Courage: Does he ever ask you to be more
harsh?

00:02:15.590 --> 00:02:19.290
Patricia: Oh yeah!

00:02:19.290 --> 00:02:24.400
Film Courage: Because I can see you not wanting
to hurt someone’s feelings?

00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:25.400
Patricia: Absolutely.

00:02:25.400 --> 00:02:29.950
And he’s like “Just be honest.

00:02:29.950 --> 00:02:33.220
Please, just tell me.”

00:02:33.220 --> 00:02:36.890
Because I do, I always think “I don’t
want to hurt anybody’s feelings.”

00:02:36.890 --> 00:02:42.300
Film Courage: I think that would be really
important to have a working relationship or

00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:45.871
a friendship where you can give them feedback
(and vice versa) and you know they are not

00:02:45.871 --> 00:02:48.010
trying to wound you to stop you.

00:02:48.010 --> 00:02:50.610
They are just trying to help make you better.

00:02:50.610 --> 00:02:51.960
That’s a rare thing to find.

00:02:51.960 --> 00:02:56.090
Patricia: Absolutely so that’s why a big
shout out to Faisal.

00:02:56.090 --> 00:03:00.870
And I think someone because they’ve kind
of read your previous work and have seen your

00:03:00.870 --> 00:03:07.010
previous films that they know what you’re
trying to do and because of that they can

00:03:07.010 --> 00:03:11.370
tell you “I think I know what you’re trying
to do but you’re maybe not doing it the

00:03:11.370 --> 00:03:13.070
best way that you could it?

00:03:13.070 --> 00:03:19.130
So let me just ask you some questions and
make some suggestions because I think I know

00:03:19.130 --> 00:03:20.550
what you’re trying to get?”

00:03:20.550 --> 00:03:23.570
I think that is very important because there
are some people who just don’t understand

00:03:23.570 --> 00:03:28.870
your work and don’t understand your films
and don’t want to are maybe not the best

00:03:28.870 --> 00:03:30.980
people to get feedback from.

00:03:30.980 --> 00:03:35.480
Film Courage: How do you know personally if
your movie idea isn’t working?

00:03:35.480 --> 00:03:38.620
Not so much if you’ve given it to someone
as a sounding board but personally?

00:03:38.620 --> 00:03:46.410
Patricia: I think it’s when you yourself
are writing it and you’re not excited by

00:03:46.410 --> 00:03:48.760
what you’re writing.

00:03:48.760 --> 00:03:55.230
I think you know that it’s working when
you’re like I really want to keep going.

00:03:55.230 --> 00:03:57.340
I personally want to know what happens next.

00:03:57.340 --> 00:04:02.510
But if you’re kind of like trudging through
it and Oh gosh I have to write another five

00:04:02.510 --> 00:04:09.320
pages or I have to write another five paragraphs
of this treatment.

00:04:09.320 --> 00:04:14.710
If you yourself are having that thought, trust
me whoever is reading it is going to also

00:04:14.710 --> 00:04:18.920
have the same thought of Oh gosh I have to
write another five pages before this is over.

00:04:18.920 --> 00:04:22.140
Film Courage: Have you ever abandoned an idea?

00:04:22.140 --> 00:04:25.170
Like you’ve been excited about it for a
little while and then realize that your heart

00:04:25.170 --> 00:04:26.170
wasn’t in it?

00:04:26.170 --> 00:04:30.720
Patricia: I think yes definitely but I think
more with like I have had that with a feature

00:04:30.720 --> 00:04:37.180
script which I was like this is not working
as a feature but I managed to kind of recycle

00:04:37.180 --> 00:04:45.280
it into something else and then also short
films like if you’ve written a short film

00:04:45.280 --> 00:04:50.041
script that was a collaboration with a friend
and then you applied to a lot of funding and

00:04:50.041 --> 00:04:55.050
you just keep getting rejected and then it’s
like well okay then let’s just not.

00:04:55.050 --> 00:04:59.020
Film Courage: So the universe was telling
you something or you just took it that way?

00:04:59.020 --> 00:05:01.820
Patricia: And then you just move onto the
next project because that got funding Okay

00:05:01.820 --> 00:05:02.820
well then I’ll just do that.

00:05:02.820 --> 00:05:03.480
Question: How do you know an idea is worth
developing into a story?

